\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $(M,g)$ be a smooth connected closed Riemannian manifold of dimension $2m$, where $m\geq1$ and closed means compact without boundary. For a two-dimensional linear subspace $\sigma\subset T_pM$, let $K_g(\sigma)$ be its sectional curvature. Write $\chi(M)=\sum_{j=0}^{2m}(-1)^j\dim_{\mathbb Q}H_j(M;\mathbb Q)$ for the Euler characteristic.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Do the following two assertions hold for every such manifold?
\[
\bigl(K_g(\sigma)>0\text{ for every }p,\sigma\bigr)\Longrightarrow\chi(M)>0,
\]
\[
\bigl(K_g(\sigma)<0\text{ for every }p,\sigma\bigr)\Longrightarrow(-1)^m\chi(M)>0.
\]
These are respectively the positive- and negative-sectional-curvature Hopf sign questions. \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.01398}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Must positive sectional curvature force positive Euler characteristic, and must negative sectional curvature force the dimension-dependent alternating sign?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 1210 (GEO-019). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Oliver Knill, \emph{Integral geometric Hopf conjectures}, arXiv:2001.01398v1 (2020), abstract and Section 1.1. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.01398}.
\end{description}
